      ],
      "nets": [
        "/PWM_OUT",
        "PWM"
      ],
      "observed": "C3 is a 100 nF capacitor connected between PWM and /PWM_OUT. The /PWM_OUT net contains only C3.1 and J2.4, so the FAN connector PWM pin has no DC path to the MCU PWM net.",
      "expected": "A fan PWM output should be DC-coupled to the fan PWM pin, either directly or through an appropriate series resistor, so the fan sees valid logic high/low levels. A series capacitor blocks DC and is not a suitable element for driving a PWM control line.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "C3 | 100nF | Capacitor_SMD:C_0805_2012Metric | Device:C_Small | Unpolarized capacitor, small symbol"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "/PWM_OUT : C3.1(passive) J2.4(Pin_4,passive)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "PWM : C3.2(passive) J4.1(MISO,passive) R2.1(passive) U2.6(PB1,bidirectional) U3.6(PB1,bidirectional)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The fan PWM pin will be AC-coupled and floating at DC; PWM control of a 4-wire fan is likely ineffective, causing the fan to run at its default/full speed or behave unpredictably.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design is otherwise a plausible ATtiny fan-controller topology, but C3 places a 100 nF capacitor in series with the fan PWM output, leaving J2 pin 4 without a DC drive path; this is a likely anomaly."
}

